WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.120
When should a brain stop collecting evidence and commit to an answer?

2
00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:13.230
The evidence comes from MT, the middle temporal area, whose neurons are tuned to

3
00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:15.370
direction. What adds it up?

4
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.440
The simplest rule is a bound.

5
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.860
Keep adding until the running total reaches a set level, then act.

6
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.920
Picture a graph, with time along the bottom and the running total up the side.

7
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.320
The total drifts upward.

8
00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:36.180
Stronger motion means a steeper drift, so the total reaches the bound sooner.

9
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:38.713
Noise adds a wobble.

10
00:00:39.493 --> 00:00:42.613
On some trials the total arrives early, and on others late.

11
00:00:43.633 --> 00:00:48.353
For a choice between two directions, draw a second bound below the start, for the

12
00:00:48.353 --> 00:00:49.353
other answer.

13
00:00:49.967 --> 00:00:54.607
On weak-motion trials, the wobble sometimes carries the total to the wrong bound.

14
00:00:55.407 --> 00:00:56.407
That is an error.

15
00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:00.350
This is a drift-to-bound model.

16
00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:11.970
Suppose some neuron held this running total.

17
00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:15.030
What would its firing do just before the eye moves?

18
00:01:21.083 --> 00:01:26.483
trials choosing the target this neuron represents, it should climb, more steeply

19
00:01:26.483 --> 00:01:27.543
when the motion is strong.

20
00:01:28.143 --> 00:01:31.403
And it should reach about the same level just before the eye moves.

21
00:01:32.283 --> 00:01:34.123
The other choice has a different endpoint.

22
00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:40.967
Now the data. Roitman and Shadlen recorded from the lateral intraparietal area, or

23
00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:42.987
LIP, in rhesus monkeys.

24
00:01:44.117 --> 00:01:48.397
Its neurons fire before an eye movement to a target in their response field.

25
00:01:50.967 --> 00:01:56.227
Averaged over many trials, the lines lie together for the first 200 milliseconds or

26
00:01:56.227 --> 00:01:57.227
so.

27
00:01:57.467 --> 00:02:02.847
Then they ramp. The ramp is steeper for stronger motion when the monkey chose the

28
00:02:02.847 --> 00:02:04.127
target in the response field.

29
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.780
Lined up to the eye movement, the firing reaches about the same rate, whatever the

30
00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.700
motion strength. The authors read this as an apparent threshold.

31
00:02:16.283 --> 00:02:18.363
These are averages over many trials.

32
00:02:19.563 --> 00:02:20.783
Single trials may look different.

33
00:02:23.783 --> 00:02:25.623
Noise also changes the timing.

34
00:02:26.523 --> 00:02:31.343
At weak motion, the wobble carries many trials to a bound early, sometimes the wrong

35
00:02:31.343 --> 00:02:32.343
one.

36
00:02:32.567 --> 00:02:34.727
With two bounds, you never wait forever.

37
00:02:35.687 --> 00:02:38.547
So mean times are shorter than a noiseless model predicts.

38
00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:41.950
Now move both bounds farther from the start.

39
00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:46.230
Choices get slower, and more accurate wherever there is net motion.

40
00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:54.910
The slowing is biggest at weak motion, where the wobble, not the drift, sets the

41
00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:55.910
time to the bound.

42
00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:01.820
A decision can be a running total that stops at a bound.

43
00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.200
The bound sets the trade between speed and accuracy.
